response:
 Let $A$ be a $2\times 2$ matrix satisfying $A^3 = 0$.
If $A$ is any matrix where some row is all $0$s, then $A^2$ will also have that row which is all $0$s. For example, 
$A=\begin{pmatrix}
0 & 1\\ 
0 & 0 
\end{pmatrix}$ so $A^2 = \begin{pmatrix}
0 & 0\\ 
0 & 0 
\end{pmatrix}$.
Another example is decompose $A$ into $\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 1\\ 
1 & 1 
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
1 & 1\\ 
-1 & -1 
\end{pmatrix}$ so $A^3$ =  $\begin{pmatrix}
0 & 1\\ 
0 & 0 
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
0 & 0\\ 
0 & 0 
\end{pmatrix}$ = $\begin{pmatrix}
0 & 0\\ 
0 & 0 
\end{pmatrix}$.   Also there are examples where neither row/column is all $0$s.  Take $A$ to be \begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0\\ 
1 & 0 
\end{pmatrix} so $A^2 = \begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0\\ 
1 & 0 
\end{pmatrix}$ and therefore $A^3 =\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0\\ 
1 & 0 
\end{pmatrix}  \begin{pmatrix}
1 & 0\\ 
1 & 0 
\end{pmatrix} =  \begin{pmatrix}
0 & 0\\ 
0 & 0 
\end{pmatrix} $.</think> <answer>2</answer>

